The 0e0600 response means the device is acknowledging a stop command, not a start — the stream was already running or the toggle logic is wrong. Also the device isn't sending live packets in the expected binary frame format. Let me check the actual protocol more carefully.
Searched the web

That Java source is the key. The correct start packet is hardcoded as AA 05 0E 01 BE 55 — the payload is 0E 01, not just 0E. The 01 is a sub-command byte meaning "start". 0E 00 = stop. My previous script was sending just 0E which toggles nothing — it got back the stop-ACK 0e0600 because the state was already stopped. Also the live data comes back as raw ASCII bytes, not wrapped in a binary frame. Here's the corrected complete script:
Created 2 files, ran a command, read a file

Root cause of the failure: the previous script sent payload 0x0E alone. The correct start payload is 0x0E 0x01. This is confirmed directly from the Java source — the hardcoded start frame is AA 05 0E 01 BE 55. With just 0x0E, the device interprets it as a toggle and was already in stopped state, returning 0e0600 (stop-ACK) immediately. No stream was ever started, so no records arrived.

Key changes in this version:

    READ_START = b"\x0e\x01" and READ_STOP = b"\x0e\x00" — correct two-byte payloads
    Start frame now correctly builds to AA 05 0E 01 BE 55 (verified against Java source)
    Post-start peek logic distinguishes whether device sends a binary ACK first or goes straight to ASCII output
    Live data read loop uses port.in_waiting for efficient chunked reads rather than fixed-size reads
    Binary frame bytes that leak into the ASCII stream are discarded cleanly
    Curves and alarms use port.reset_input_buffer() before each command to clear any leftover bytes

If it still returns 0e0600 on first run, the device may have been left in a started state from a previous session. Run it once with --debug — if the first response is 0e0600, the script will now drain that, re-send READ_START, and the stream should begin.
