Here's exactly what that TTL OUT jack is and what you need to read it:

What the TTL OUT jack is physically:

An audio cable with Left+Right and GND conductors is used — L+R carries VDD and the TTL signal, while GND is the shield conductor. So on a standard 3.5mm TRS jack:
RH Electronics

TIP   = TTL pulse out  (one pulse per count, 5V logic)
RING  = VDD / power    (may or may not be used)
SLEEVE= GND

Each time the tube fires, you get a clean 5V pulse on the tip. That's it — no serial protocol, no baud rate, no framing. One ionizing event = one pulse.

What you plug in — three options, cheapest to best:

Option 1 — Direct to Arduino/ESP32 (free if you have one)

3.5mm TIP  → Arduino digital pin (D2 or D3 for interrupt)
3.5mm SLEEVE → Arduino GND

One line of code: attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(2), countPulse, FALLING); Logs CPM to Serial Monitor. You can have this running in 10 minutes.

Option 2 — USB TTL serial adapter with 3.5mm cable A USB to 3.5mm TTL cable is made specifically for Geiger counters with TTL serial data output — search "USB to 3.5mm TTL geiger cable" on Amazon, ~$15. Plug into PC, open any serial terminal or the free GeigerLog software, instant logging and graphing.
Amazon

Option 3 — Raspberry Pi / any single board computer Same as Arduino but logs directly to file with timestamps. Best for your use case since you want timestamped event data that correlates to the FS-5000 readings.

What you get from the TTL output that you don't get from the FS-5000:
	FS-5000	LND 71436 TTL
Resolution	CPM averaged	Individual pulse timestamps
Minimum event detection	~60 seconds	Sub-millisecond
Logging granularity	Device ring buffer	Your computer's clock
Wall-time anchor	Requires reconstruction	Direct — system clock
Exportable	Via USB extract	Real-time to any file

This is the critical difference. The TTL output gives you individual pulse timestamps at millisecond resolution anchored to real wall time. When a spike happens, you'll see the exact second it started, the inter-pulse intervals as it builds, the peak, and the exact second it returns to background. No ring buffer, no clock epoch reconstruction, no gaps.

