cmd

python comdump.py --port COM3

That's it. Every byte that comes off COM3 goes straight to disk. No protocol awareness. No framing. No stopping at packet boundaries. If the device is mid-transmission when you connect, you get mid-transmission bytes. If it's idle, you get whatever it sends when it decides to send. Counter shows bytes received and throughput in real time.

Ctrl+C stops it and immediately writes three files:

    COM3_TIMESTAMP.bin — raw bytes, byte 0 to last byte received
    COM3_TIMESTAMP.hex.txt — offset/hex/ascii, nothing else
    COM3_TIMESTAMP.csv — one row per byte

Other options:
cmd

python comdump.py --list                          # see all ports
python comdump.py --port COM3 --baud 9600         # different baud
python comdump.py --port COM3 --out fs5000.bin    # named output
python comdump.py --port /dev/ttyUSB0             # Linux
python comdump.py --port COM3 --no-csv            # skip csv if large

Works on anything — FS-5000, LND, Arduino, Raspberry Pi, anything talking serial. Baud is the only thing you need to match.
