Easiest possible DIY — you probably already have everything.

What you need:

A USB-to-TTL serial adapter. The CH340, CP2102, or FT232 chips — same family as what's in the FS-5000 itself. On eBay or AliExpress they're $1-3 shipped. Amazon has them for $6-8 if you need it faster. Search "CH340 USB TTL adapter" or "CP2102 USB serial."

If you have any of these already sitting around they all work:

    Arduino Uno/Nano (use it as a USB-serial passthrough)
    Raspberry Pi (built-in UART)
    ESP8266/ESP32
    Any old Arduino clone

The wiring is three solder points:

LND TTL jack (3.5mm TRS)
  TIP    → RX pin on your adapter
  SLEEVE → GND on your adapter
  RING   → ignore it or tie to VCC if needed for power

That's the whole circuit. The adapter already handles the USB side.

If you have an Arduino Uno right now:

LND TIP    → Arduino pin D2
LND SLEEVE → Arduino GND

Upload this:
cpp

volatile unsigned long count = 0;
volatile unsigned long last_us = 0;

void countPulse() {
  count++;
  last_us = micros();
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(2), countPulse, FALLING);
}

void loop() {
  static unsigned long last_report = 0;
  if (millis() - last_report >= 1000) {
    Serial.print(millis());
    Serial.print(",");
    Serial.println(count);
    last_report = millis();
  }
}

Outputs timestamp_ms, cumulative_count every second. Pipe that into comdump.py pointed at the Arduino's COM port and you have a timestamped pulse log to disk.

If you have literally nothing — a 3.5mm to bare-wire cable cut from any old pair of headphones, tip wire to any 5V logic input you have. The signal is just a clean 5V pulse. Anything that can read a digital GPIO can count it.

